export default "<p class=\"article-lead\">Every week I get some version of the same message. It comes from CEOs running €500M businesses. From CFOs at global banks. From CMOs with 200-person marketing organizations. Smart, capable, accomplished people who are genuinely stuck on one question: \"Where do we actually start?\"</p>\n\n        <p>Not \"should we do AI?\" They're past that.</p>\n\n        <p>Not \"does AI have ROI?\" They've seen the numbers.</p>\n\n        <p>The question is always the same: <em>What does Day 1 look like? What do I actually do on Monday morning?</em></p>\n\n        <p>This week, I'm going to answer that with unusual specificity.</p>\n\n        <p><strong>A day-by-day guide for your first week of taking AI seriously.</strong></p>\n\n        <p>Five days. Real actions. No budget required.</p>\n\n        <hr>\n\n        <h3>This Week in AI</h3>\n\n        <div class=\"ai-news-placeholder\">\n          <div class=\"ai-news-placeholder-icon\">\n            <svg width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"><path d=\"M13 2L3 14h9l-1 8 10-12h-9l1-8z\"/></svg>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"ai-news-placeholder-text\">\n            <strong>This Week in AI</strong>\n            This section updates with the latest AI news each week, developments translated into plain business impact for leaders.\n          </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <hr>\n\n        <p>Now for this week's deep dive...</p>\n\n        <h3>The Numbers Behind the Urgency</h3>\n\n        <p><strong>JPMorgan has 250,000 employees registered on their internal AI platform.</strong> 60% of them use it daily. This is not a small experiment at the innovation lab. This is a quarter million people incorporating AI into their normal work, every day.</p>\n\n        <p><strong>Companies with formal AI strategies succeed at implementation 80% of the time. Companies without them succeed 37% of the time.</strong> That 43-percentage-point gap is one of the largest performance differentials in any major technology adoption study I've seen.</p>\n\n        <p><strong>48% of employees say that formal training would meaningfully increase their AI usage.</strong> Nearly half your team is ready to move. They are waiting for structure, permission, and a starting point.</p>\n\n        <p>You have the power to give them all three. This week.</p>\n\n        <hr>\n\n        <h3>The Day-by-Day Playbook</h3>\n\n        <p>Here is your week. Block the time now. Don't treat this as \"if I get to it.\" Schedule it.</p>\n\n        <h4>Monday: Use AI yourself. One hour. A real task.</h4>\n\n        <p>This is the most important day of the week because it's the one most leaders skip.</p>\n\n        <p>Set aside one hour. Close Outlook. Put your phone down. Pick one real task that's sitting in your inbox, an email you've been avoiding, a document you need to synthesize, a brief you need to write.</p>\n\n        <p>Open an AI tool, ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, it doesn't matter which one, and use it to actually do the task.</p>\n\n        <p>That forty-five minutes of firsthand experience will make you a better leader of this transformation than any briefing document ever written.</p>\n\n        <h4>Tuesday: Survey your team. Two questions.</h4>\n\n        <p>Send a note to your direct reports with exactly two questions:</p>\n\n        <p><em>\"What AI tools are you currently using, for anything, personal or professional?\"</em></p>\n\n        <p><em>\"What are the three most repetitive tasks in your current role?\"</em></p>\n\n        <p>Your team is already using AI. Widely, quietly, and often without telling you. And the repetitive tasks they describe are almost certainly your highest-ROI AI opportunities.</p>\n\n        <h4>Wednesday: Pick three opportunities.</h4>\n\n        <p>Take what you learned on Tuesday and apply one filter.</p>\n\n        <p>You're looking for opportunities that meet three criteria simultaneously: <strong>high impact on real work, low risk if something goes wrong, and measurable within 90 days.</strong></p>\n\n        <p>Three is plenty. Three is actually ambitious if you execute on them properly. The temptation will be to pick twelve. Resist it.</p>\n\n        <p><strong>Constraint is the friend of execution.</strong></p>\n\n        <h4>Thursday: Name your AI champions.</h4>\n\n        <p>Every company has people who others turn to when something new comes along. They're not always the most technically sophisticated people. They're the most <strong>connected</strong> people, the ones whose enthusiasm is contagious, whose credibility is trusted.</p>\n\n        <p>Think about who those people are. Name them. Three to five people.</p>\n\n        <p>You're not creating a committee. You're identifying the people through whom change actually travels.</p>\n\n        <h4>Friday: Tell everyone.</h4>\n\n        <p>Not in a vague, aspirational, \"exciting AI journey ahead\" way.</p>\n\n        <p>Specifically.</p>\n\n        <p><strong>This is what we're going to work on.</strong> (The three opportunities from Wednesday, named specifically.)</p>\n\n        <p><strong>This is why it matters.</strong> (Connect it to your team's work and your customers' experience.)</p>\n\n        <p><strong>This is the goal we're holding ourselves to in the next 90 days.</strong> (A concrete, measurable target.)</p>\n\n        <p>Transparency is not weakness here. It's the mechanism. When people know specifically what you're aiming at, they can contribute to it.</p>\n\n        <hr>\n\n        <div class=\"monks-story\">\n          <span class=\"monks-story-label\">Monks Success Story</span>\n          <p>When Monks committed to AI as a core capability, it didn't start with a software selection.</p>\n          <p>It started with a structural decision.</p>\n          <p>We collapsed from a holding company with multiple brands into a single, unified organization. We made <strong>Wesley ter Haar</strong>, our co-founder, both Chief AI Officer and Chief Revenue Officer simultaneously. That dual role was deliberate. It said: AI is not a technology function living in IT. It is a revenue function.</p>\n          <p>We built <strong>Monks Foundry</strong>, a team of 150 NVIDIA-certified engineers embedded directly into our production infrastructure.</p>\n          <p>We assembled a <strong>50-person Agentic Advisory team</strong> dedicated entirely to helping clients build AI-native operating models.</p>\n          <p>None of that happened in the first week. But the first week was a decision. Not a budget. Not a vendor. Not a consultant engagement.</p>\n          <p><strong>A decision.</strong></p>\n        </div>\n\n        <blockquote>\n          <p>\"The biggest myth about AI transformation is that you need to have it all figured out before you start. You don't. You just need to start.\"</p>\n        </blockquote>\n\n        <hr>\n\n        <div class=\"action-callout\">\n          <div class=\"action-callout-label\">\n            <svg width=\"18\" height=\"18\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"><path d=\"M13 2L3 14h9l-1 8 10-12h-9l1-8z\"/></svg>\n            Your One Action This Week\n          </div>\n          <p>You have the playbook. You know the five days.</p>\n          <p>So here is the only question that matters: <strong>Which Monday are you starting on?</strong></p>\n          <p>Because \"we'll get to this in Q3\" is a choice. It's just not the choice I'd recommend.</p>\n          <p>One hour. One AI tool. One real task.</p>\n          <p><strong>This Monday. Go.</strong></p>\n        </div>";
